//! check: run(exit=3)
//! phase: mem
//! conforms: gram.pat.or
//!
//! wolf-lang#1061: both alternatives bind `x` at `int`, so the arm sees
//! one `x`, holding what the matching alternative bound
//! ([gram.pat.or]). The checker accepts it. The compiled machines'
//! join of an or-pattern's bindings is still being built, so they
//! refuse the arm at `mem` as "cannot compile this yet" (the
//! conservatism ledger); lupin runs it (exit 3).

enum E { A(int), B(int) }

fn f(v: E) -> int {
    match v {
        A(x) | B(x) => x,
    }
}

fn main() -> int { f(E.B(3)) }
